00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:25.220
Yeah. Is is there a way to get morale back?

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:28.080
I don't know. I doubt it because I don't think so.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:34.980
From what I from what the manual makes it sound like your morale is directly tied to like how many civilians are still alive.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:38.780
Or like if you if you've killed six civilians, you're out of morale and you lose.

00:17:39.140 --> 00:17:41.960
So why isn't that number just called civilians?

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:44.540
Why is it called morale?

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:46.040
Why are we translating it to morale?

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:50.580
I saw like five. Maybe I saw four or five civilians in the entire game.

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:56.800
So I don't. But that would check out right though, Sean, because the morale is only available in the first level anyway.

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:59.560
It's no longer a thing after the first stage.

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:00.900
So. Yeah, you're right.

00:18:00.980 --> 00:18:08.180
I don't know. But it is weird, though, that they would have an endless amount of enemies, but only six civilians programmed into the game.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:11.260
Well, there's no civilians left because everyone's fighting in the war.

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:12.340
They're not civilians. They're soldiers.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:14.180
They've been drafted. There you go.

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:16.140
There's only six people that aren't fighting in the war.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:17.680
They're all grandmas.

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:23.460
OK, so like where the civilians are is like the town at the end of level one.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:32.620
Like you you you go, you find the explosives, you blow up a bridge doesn't actually blow up, but you you agree that it blows up.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:37.480
And then you continue to the to the town.

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:40.160
And this looks like there's a bunch of like empty hearts.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:41.280
You have to walk into the huts.

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:46.580
It shows like some weird like background stuff.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:49.700
And I just kept walking in and out of these huts.

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:55.740
But apparently these these these interactable objects look like they're just part of the background.

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:58.900
But you have to you have to like interact with them by pressing up.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:05.280
And I just couldn't understand that for the like, I don't know if this was more if this felt more intuitive to you, Mike.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:13.800
Like what? But I was just stuck in this empty village for like 10 minutes because I didn't understand how to interact with anything.

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:22.360
Yeah. And you actually have to you have to interact with it in order to get to the next stage to find the tunnel system itself in one of the village huts.

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:26.740
But, you know, full disclosure, even though I got through this game, I used a guide.

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:27.760
I used a guide, too.

00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:35.060
I can't imagine how this stuff just like even even just navigating through the mess that is the first jungle level.

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:39.460
I can't imagine how that's accessible to any gamer, you know?

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:42.140
Yeah. But but, you know, it is what it is.

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:51.520
And I would say that after all of the hurdles that you had to overcome just to get to that point, you're probably inclined to figure it out, you know, like as a player.

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:57.100
Sure. It's not excusable, but it's like, you know, why were you expecting it to be simple in the first place?

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:14.060
True. I am glad, though, that you mentioned the pressing up there because that reminds me of something, you know, before you get to the huts or even just when you're on the regular side scrolling parts that really bothers me is that up is to jump.

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:32.660
And I and I think that that coupled with the weird like frame perfect, not really, but almost frame perfect feeling things you have to do in order to avoid enemies and the weird controls in general makes it feel just uncomfortable and difficult and like not intuitive to move around.

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:39.540
Like sometimes I'm trying to jump and go forward, but I guess I'm not holding the forward enough and I just jump and I stay still and I get shot anyways.

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:41.680
And I don't know.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:46.860
There's something weird to me about using up as jump when you can just use B as jump.

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:50.320
I know B is mapped to grenades, but grenades are useless anyways.

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:52.840
So just put them on up like it just feels weird.

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:53.840
You can't.

00:20:53.920 --> 00:21:00.480
It's so much harder to hit to like hit to direct hit one direction and jump at the same time when they're on the same deep.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:09.320
Well, Joe, as one of the sociopaths that uses up to jump and smash brothers, I will say that it wasn't as terrible for me.

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:15.540
But I do agree that it but when I was just trying to move forward, I would sometimes jump.

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:19.240
And when I was trying to jump, I would sometimes just move forward.

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:21.860
So I agree that it was bad implementation.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:32.780
Right. And I'll say maybe maybe by smash brothers, we've gameplay has gotten smooth enough where it's maybe maybe a little better to use up up as jump than than back in 1988.

00:21:32.780 --> 00:21:43.120
Joe, it's funny you brought it up, though, because I remember like for some reason having difficulty remembering that the up button is jump as I was playing this game.

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:48.520
And and I threw grenades more often than I than I wanted to because I meant to jump.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:51.620
And there's something not responsive about that.

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:52.800
So I think you're on to something.

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:54.000
I don't know exactly why.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:22:02.060
I'm not I'm not against games having the D pad up button to jump like we've seen that in some other games.

00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:08.520
And we wish that there would have been like a C button, if you will, so that you could have mapped jump to a button instead.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:09.960
But I'm not against it.

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:15.100
For some reason, though, in this game, it just didn't feel appropriate to be like up on the D pad.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:23.980
I think a big part of it for me, I mean, not to harp on this, but I really the more I'm thinking about it, the more I realize that grenades are a completely useless item in this game.

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:26.980
It is it is 100 percent of the time.

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:30.240
Better to just shoot your gun.

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:32.440
One, you'll still kill whatever you hit in one hit.

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:34.740
It'll shoot straight forward and just hit them.

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:37.100
You don't have to aim like in some weird arc.

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:43.920
It doesn't take literally two and a half seconds for you to go through your animation to throw the grenade and you don't run out of ammo.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:45.980
Why would you ever throw grenades in this game?

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:52.520
And you know what? The up button is also used to navigate throughout the jungle to to the different screens.

00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:53.960
You know, you have the up and down.

00:22:53.960 --> 00:23:03.860
So I guess that's another reason why it just doesn't feel quite correct to have it like double mapped to like exploration and jumping because you're not jumping in that particular instance.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:06.380
Right. And selecting items in the huts.

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:16.420
I mean, we have seen I mean, we have seen up as interact before, even as early as Castlevania.

00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:19.840
Like that was how you would enter doors and stuff.

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:26.180
So, I mean, neither usage is unprecedented, but I guess together it just sort of feels messy.

00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:33.800
Well, don't forget, like also in Castlevania up plus your attack is how you use your specials attacks, you know, like dagger and stuff.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:34.940
So like that's even weirder.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.100
Yeah. But we accept that for some reason.

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:41.060
Well, it feels better. I want games to only use the up button.

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:43.520
I want there to be a game where the up button does everything.

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:46.200
It's like quap, but it's just Q.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:50.180
I just want a controller that's just an up button.

00:23:50.280 --> 00:24:20.980
Yeah, there you go. I want a controller that's just an up button.

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:28.660
All right, so that's stage one.

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:30.980
Let's now talk about stage two.

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.820
Remember, a completely different game now, okay?

00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:38.260
It's no longer this action side-scrolling, not platformer,

00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:42.100
but action side-scroller that we were talking about just a second ago.

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:47.100
Now we're playing a first-person shooter in a tunnel system,

00:24:47.570 --> 00:24:49.760
and you have to kind of navigate your way through this,

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:53.140
and you've got to collect flares and a compass

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:56.380
and try to get out of this tunnel.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:59.720
And I will say, to the game's credit,

00:25:00.520 --> 00:25:07.160
this idea of putting the map side-by-side with the gameplay is incredible

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:14.100
and also probably a little tricky on their side of a way to use the NES's power

00:25:14.100 --> 00:25:19.200
because they clearly make the screen that your gameplay is on.

00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:23.940
They make that smaller so that they're able to probably use more calculus.

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:25.240
We've seen that before.

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:28.820
Yeah. I mean, I don't really think it's – I don't think it had anything to do with –

00:25:28.820 --> 00:25:31.340
because when we've seen that in more recent games,

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:33.900
you're talking about Doom on Super NES,

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:39.020
which is all about conserving processing power because it is a 3D render.

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:42.620
I think this is just so that it actually has more space on the screen.

00:25:43.080 --> 00:25:45.220
You didn't think some of those sprites looked really good, though?

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:47.360
Like those guys who come out of water with the knives?

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:48.320
Oh, yeah. They did look good.

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:54.600
Like this is – like of all of the first-person segments in these video games

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:57.920
that we've played, I think this is the best executed one

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:02.080
because moving is smooth when you're moving.

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:11.920
You do have that map, so you're not going to get completely lost like in Goonies

00:26:11.920 --> 00:26:13.280
or whatever else.

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.220
Just to know what side of the wall you're facing, though, too.

00:26:16.340 --> 00:26:18.120
Like that is a big thing for people to overcome.

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:19.400
You have your kernel directions.

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:24.180
I know computer gamers at the time were playing a lot of first-person games like that,

00:26:24.260 --> 00:26:26.160
and they probably were used to it by now,

00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:29.740
but you have to also remember that the NES is like a family system,

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:31.740
probably mostly geared towards young kids.

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:37.600
Oh, yeah, very different audiences. It's very hard to understand which wall you're facing for someone even like us,

00:26:37.700 --> 00:26:39.400
you know, who didn't grow up on those computer games,

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:41.140
let alone like an 8-year-old kid.

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:45.800
I think having the arrow not even just show you where you are on the map,

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:49.020
but also what direction you're facing in is really helpful.

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:51.480
That was the first thing that stood out to me by far,

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:55.040
because again, to call it back to Golgo 13, which had a section like this,

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:58.780
which I thought was really cool, but it was very flawed

00:26:58.780 --> 00:27:00.940
because you have no idea which way you're facing,

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:04.600
and if you press an input by mistake one time and you miss it

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:06.700
and you don't keep track of it, you're completely lost

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:08.980
because every direction you look looks exactly the same.

00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:12.040
Granted, this every direction you look looks exactly the same,

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:13.940
but it looks a little nicer,

00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:16.400
and you're always glancing over to that arrow,

00:27:16.420 --> 00:27:19.140
so you're actually navigating and you know where you're going.

00:27:19.400 --> 00:27:21.380
Yeah, I was actually staring at the arrow

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:23.540
until I noticed I wasn't moving forward anymore,

00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:25.820
and that meant that I had to interact with an enemy,

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:30.040
and that's where I think the experience gets a little weird

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:34.360
because they always put your cursor in a pretty weird spot

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:37.200
if you had interacted with the cursor recently,

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:42.720
so sometimes you needed super quick reaction

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:47.040
just to kill the enemy before it kills you.

00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:50.800
And John, you're talking about the crosshair, right?

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:53.320
The crosshair. No, I'm just making sure,

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:56.240
because the weird thing is that as you're walking,

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:58.820
it swings to the left and right,

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:01.000
and I guess it's trying to...

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:03.340
Exactly. But that's kind of weird, right?

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:06.400
Mm-hmm. As a gameplay design choice,

00:28:06.400 --> 00:28:09.240
because even in modern games,

00:28:09.400 --> 00:28:10.340
you know, like Call of Duty,

00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:12.960
it might move a little bit from the center, right?

00:28:13.040 --> 00:28:14.040
Like, ever so slightly,

00:28:14.180 --> 00:28:17.140
but it doesn't swing rapidly as you walk.

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:20.780
Yeah, I do think it's an interesting touch

00:28:20.780 --> 00:28:23.380
because it does at least make it feel like

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:24.940
you're a person in a space

00:28:24.940 --> 00:28:26.680
and not so much just a floating camera

00:28:26.680 --> 00:28:27.660
going down a corridor.

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:35.100
Sure. But it's still an odd choice.

00:28:36.740 --> 00:28:38.880
I assume that the goal of that

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:39.760
is because otherwise,

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:42.760
all the enemies appear right in the center of the screen,

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:43.880
and if your cursor is immediately

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:44.700
in the center of the screen,

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:46.160
you just got... Yeah, not all of them,

00:28:46.220 --> 00:28:48.060
but yeah, for most of them,

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:49.460
center of the screen. For the most part.

00:28:49.620 --> 00:28:51.480
Okay, gotcha. I thought it was all of them,

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:52.920
but yeah, so even if a lot of them

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:54.140
appear in the middle of the screen,

00:28:54.240 --> 00:28:55.540
that makes a lot of them really easy

00:28:55.540 --> 00:28:56.720
to just immediately kill,

00:28:57.320 --> 00:28:59.300
whereas I guess they were trying to make it

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:01.100
so you have to still aim.

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:03.540
So like, oh, your most weird cursor is going to be

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:04.500
when an enemy shows up

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:05.860
and then you got to aim it.

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:07.840
Maybe a little bit of a...

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:10.500
Not the best executed way of doing that,

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:13.720
but it's probably better than just always...

00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:16.960
No, for sure. Pretty much like auto-locking.

00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:19.680
Having some randomization, I guess,

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:22.240
in where your default position is

00:29:22.840 --> 00:29:24.400
makes it a little bit more challenging,

00:29:24.400 --> 00:29:26.060
but that is also sort of...

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:29.360
My other complaint about this segment

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:31.660
is that I don't think it's

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:34.400
all that well-balanced for...

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:37.640
So you're going to be...

00:29:38.660 --> 00:29:39.660
You're in a maze.

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:41.340
So, of course,

00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:42.800
if you know the route of the maze,

00:29:42.940 --> 00:29:43.920
it's going to be pretty easy,

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:46.620
but since we most likely

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:47.880
don't know the route of the maze,

00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:49.380
you're going to be going into

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:50.800
a lot of places that you don't need to go,

00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:53.940
and that means that

00:29:53.940 --> 00:29:56.180
with your limited ammo supply

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:57.260
in this segment,

00:29:57.860 --> 00:30:00.280
you could just easily fail this

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:01.300
just by...

00:30:01.300 --> 00:30:02.960
You could basically soft-lock yourself

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:04.640
if you're too far away from the exit

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:06.840
and you get into

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:08.580
too many

00:30:11.180 --> 00:30:12.260
fighting segments

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:15.800
that you just won't have any ammo

00:30:15.800 --> 00:30:17.040
and you won't be able to defend yourself

00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:21.380
and then you're just done. I think that it would have been nicer

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:22.760
if maybe...

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:24.160
Because I know that in some of these spaces

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:25.520
you can pick up more ammo,

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:27.760
but if they were a little bit more

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:29.260
generous with those,

00:30:29.860 --> 00:30:32.180
this would have been a bit more enjoyable

00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:34.420
to play through. Yeah, I think it feels weird

00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:35.600
to all of a sudden add

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:37.060
a realism element,

00:30:37.060 --> 00:30:39.120
like an ammo count here,

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:40.540
as opposed to...

00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:42.160
Because it wasn't in stage one.

00:30:42.360 --> 00:30:44.360
And also just the fact that...

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:46.340
Sure, now you have this limited amount of ammo,

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:48.960
but enemies don't stop spawning either.

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:50.320
You could go back...

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:52.700
You could be like, oh, maybe I was supposed to go back that way,

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:54.080
retreat your steps,

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:55.220
and then find like,

00:30:55.620 --> 00:30:57.140
nope, these are just the same...

00:30:57.140 --> 00:30:59.400
Nothing was there, but I have to deal with the same enemies again.

00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:02.500
And thankfully they are nice

00:31:02.500 --> 00:31:04.280
with the amount of hits that you're allowed to collect.

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:06.740
I feel like... Four. I feel like you could take four

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:07.680
and that's pretty good.

00:31:08.240 --> 00:31:10.260
Yeah. Some games would be like one

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:12.480
and you're dead. Well, it's also a situation

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:13.360
where I...

00:31:13.360 --> 00:31:14.700
When I was playing through this,

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:16.920
I went to collect a flare

00:31:16.920 --> 00:31:17.860
because you're looking for

00:31:17.860 --> 00:31:19.160
flares and a compass

00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:22.180
in this, and then you can exit

00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:23.140
through another ladder.

00:31:24.700 --> 00:31:26.200
But I realized

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:27.300
that there was a flare

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:28.140
on the other side of the map,

00:31:28.160 --> 00:31:29.700
so I went and got the flare.

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:31.240
While I was going there,

00:31:31.240 --> 00:31:32.340
I ran out of ammo,

00:31:32.900 --> 00:31:33.800
picked up the flare.

00:31:34.020 --> 00:31:34.960
On my way back,

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:36.220
I ran into an enemy.

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:37.820
There's nothing you can do

00:31:37.820 --> 00:31:38.660
about that anymore,

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:40.060
so you just let them hit you.

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:42.460
Like, okay, I've got one hit.

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:45.700
Then it immediately spawned

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:46.620
him in front of me again.

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:48.900
Two hits. And then immediately spawned

